# PROJECT STANDARDS RULES

## Purpose

This rule defines the standards every project must follow when being designed, built, reviewed, and maintained.

A project is not successful because it compiles.

A successful project is:

* structured
* understandable
* maintainable
* scalable
* production-ready

---

# Core Principle

## Build Systems, Not Features

Every implementation decision must consider:

```text id="x7m4qa"
Current Requirement

+

Future Growth

+

Maintenance Cost

+

User Experience
```

Avoid solutions that only work for the immediate task.

---

# Project Understanding

Before making changes:

Understand:

* project purpose
* existing architecture
* technology stack
* file structure
* design system
* business requirements

Do not introduce patterns that conflict with the project.

---

# Architecture Standards

Projects should have:

* clear boundaries
* logical organization
* predictable structure
* separation of concerns

Prefer:

```text id="m9v3qx"
Presentation

↓

Business Logic

↓

Data Layer

↓

Infrastructure
```

---

# File Organization

Files should be:

* named clearly
* grouped logically
* easy to locate

Avoid:

* random placement
* duplicate files
* unclear naming

---

# Naming Standards

Use names that communicate purpose.

Good:

```text id="p4m8qa"
UserProfileCard

OrderService

PaymentController
```

Avoid:

```text id="k6v2mz"
Component1

HelperNew

TempFile
```

---

# Configuration Standards

Configuration should be:

* centralized
* documented
* secure

Never:

* hardcode secrets
* duplicate environment values
* expose private configuration

---

# Environment Management

Separate:

* development
* testing
* production

Ensure:

* environment variables are clear
* deployment requirements are documented
* sensitive values are protected

---

# Dependency Standards

Before adding dependencies:

Evaluate:

```text id="v8m5qa"
Need

↓

Security

↓

Maintenance

↓

Performance Impact
```

Avoid:

* unnecessary packages
* duplicate solutions
* abandoned tools

---

# Documentation Standards

Important systems should document:

* purpose
* setup
* architecture
* usage
* limitations

Documentation should help another developer continue the project.

---

# Feature Development Standards

Every feature should define:

## Purpose

Why does this exist?

---

## User Value

Who benefits?

---

## Technical Requirements

How should it work?

---

## Completion Criteria

How do we know it is finished?

---

# Quality Before Completion

Before marking work complete:

Verify:

```text id="c8n4vx"
✓ Feature works

✓ Edge cases considered

✓ Errors handled

✓ Responsive behavior works

✓ Accessibility considered

✓ Code is maintainable

✓ Documentation updated
```

---

# Testing Standards

Test:

* important workflows
* business-critical logic
* reusable components
* failure scenarios

Do not assume:

"it works on my machine."

---

# Security Standards

Every project must consider:

* authentication
* authorization
* data protection
* input validation
* secret management

Security is part of quality.

---

# Performance Standards

Consider:

* loading speed
* rendering efficiency
* network usage
* resource consumption

Optimize based on user impact.

---

# Delivery Standards

Before delivery:

Confirm:

```text id="z2m7qa"
Production readiness

↓

Quality review

↓

Documentation

↓

Deployment confidence
```

---

# Forbidden Practices

Never introduce:

```text id="f6q8mv"
❌ Temporary hacks

❌ Fake implementations

❌ Untracked shortcuts

❌ Unnecessary complexity

❌ Broken conventions

❌ Ignored errors
```

---

# Final Rule

Every project should be built as if it will be maintained, expanded, and trusted by real users.

Do not create disposable code.

Create durable products.
